content.text:
 I can see the problem clearly.

The `onConnect` listener is receiving the port but doing nothing meaningful with it:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onConnect.addListener((port) => {
  if (port.name === 'keepalive') {
    port.onDisconnect.addListener(() => {});  // empty handler
  }
});
```

An empty disconnect handler does not hold the port. Edge sees a port with no active listeners doing real work and suspends the worker anyway. The port needs to be stored in a variable so the service worker maintains a reference to it. Without that reference the garbage collector drops it and the connection closes.

The fix is two lines:

input.command:
cp /mnt/user-data/uploads/background.js /home/claude/background_fixed.js

# Fix the onConnect handler to actually hold the port reference
python3 << 'EOF'
with open('/home/claude/background_fixed.js', 'r') as f:
    content = f.read()

old = """// Keep-alive port from content scripts — prevents service worker suspension
chrome.runtime.onConnect.addListener((port) => {
  if (port.name === 'keepalive') {
    port.onDisconnect.addListener(() => {});
  }
});"""

new = """// Keep-alive port from content scripts — prevents service worker suspension
// CRITICAL: ports must be stored in a Set or Edge drops the reference and suspends anyway
const keepAlivePorts = new Set();
chrome.runtime.onConnect.addListener((port) => {
  if (port.name === 'keepalive') {
    keepAlivePorts.add(port);
    port.onDisconnect.addListener(() => {
      keepAlivePorts.delete(port);
    });
  }
});"""

if old in content:
    content = content.replace(old, new)
    with open('/home/claude/background_fixed.js', 'w') as f:
        f.write(content)
    print("Fixed successfully")
else:
    print("Pattern not found - check spacing")
EOF

